`stdio.h` – Provides standard input and output functions.
`unistd.h` – Provides system functions such as `sleep()`.
`main()` – The entry point from where C program execution begins.
`while(1)` – Creates an infinite loop that runs continuously.
`i++` – Increments the variable value by one.
`printf()` – Prints output on the terminal.
`sleep(1)` – Stops execution for one second.
`gcc` – Compiler used to convert C source code into an executable file.
`&` – Executes a program in the background.
`top` – Monitors active processes along with their CPU and memory usage.
`top -u username` – Shows processes belonging to a particular user.
`top -p PID` – Displays information about a specific process.
`top -d 1` – Refreshes process details at one-second intervals.
`PID` – Unique identification number assigned to a process.
`%CPU` – Indicates the percentage of CPU used by a process.
`%MEM` – Indicates the percentage of memory used by a process.
`jobs` – Lists currently running or stopped background jobs in the shell.
`kill` – Sends a signal to stop or control a process.
`q` – Exits the `top` monitoring interface.
